  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nPrecipitation chances will diminish during the day Sunday from west \nto east. Monday is expected to be mostly dry but that dry weather \nwill be short lived as another potent system impacts the state. An \nupper low pressure system will dig into southern California on \nMonday then will transition into Arizona and New Mexico Monday night \nand Tuesday. That will create an amplified southwest flow over Iowa \nand create a Monsoonal flow moisture connection. While this is \nongoing, a good connection to Gulf moisture will also be established \nwith the two moisture sources overlapping across Iowa and push PWAT \nvalues back over 2\", which is climatologically significant for late \nSeptember. Shower and storm potential will increase Monday night and \npersist into Tuesday night. Heavy rainfall is possible which could \nfurther exacerbate hydrology conditions. Still monitoring the \nWednesday and Thursday timeframe as the upper low translates into \nthe Kansas region. Also during this timeframe, monitoring the \npotential for Hurricane Polo being ingested into the southwest flow \nahead of the upper low, which is likely dependent on if that \ntropical system can maintain a northeast path across the Baja \nCalifornia. Should this occur, it could be a source for more \nheavy rainfall mid-week.",
